A||N||9||FieroJoe||Scary high idle w/ AC!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||06-09-2001||05:51 PM||||87 DIS 2.5L, 5spd tranny.<P>Today I was driving down the road, and turned on the AC while just cruising along at 55mph, when I pushed in the clutch to take it out of gear to coast, the idle went way up, up to 2700rpm, and it steadly went up to just past 3k.  This has done this before, but it usually immediatly comes down to normal idle speed (just under 1000).  This time though, it didnt.  Just kept going that way.  Well, it was freaking me out so I turned the AC off and it went right back down to where it should.  I tried it again, and it went right back up!<P>I pulled over, and let it sit there while the car wasnt in motion (when I sometimes get a high idle without the ac on, if the car is stopped it comes down, but never, ever when the car is in motion)  It never came down, so I just turned it off and then went to work.<P>After work, I drove home without it, and then, after the car was warmed up, I let it idle and took off the air cleaner cover so I could see the fuel injector.  I turned on the AC, and of course the revvs went right up there, but after about 40 seconds they slowly came down, and then the idle went back down to normal (AC still on).  I was looking at the butterfly, just to see if the butterfly was opening up or not.  It wasnt, but I could tell that the ECM was throwing a whole lot more of fuel into there.  It made a super high hissing sound too, probably because it was getting a lot of fuel and trying to suck it through a very tiny opening (from the butterfly being at idle state).<P>So, what the heck?  ogre, do you think this may be another thing that'd be fixed by the PROM we've discussed?  It seems as if it could be similar to the idle problems we talked about, the ECM giving too much fuel for the amount the throttle butterfly is opened.<P>All vac lines are brand new, new MAT sensor, new TPS, new gasket between TBI and air cleaner.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||06-09-2001||08:56 PM||||I don't know... I gave you the link to get the TSB. I don't remember any mention of the AC being involved. You'll still have to find a scanner to apply the TSB.<P>AC does provide a signal to the ECM, so the computer does know when it's on. I don't have any details on the programing of just what it does.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||06-09-2001||09:22 PM||||Yeah, I have been trying to rule out as many things as possible before taking the car in to get scanned, and seeing if I could get the new PROM.  I have read that TSB, and will be bringing it with me when I get the PROM scanned.<P>Though I am not very familiar with AC systems in general, could it be one of the pressure switches not giving a signal to stop compressing or something like that?  Where are they located on the compressor?  I could get under there and make sure the connections are tight.<P>The air was recharged last year about this time, and it is still pretty cold, if that makes any difference.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||06-09-2001||11:04 PM||||Funny (not in a humorous way).  Mine does just the opposite.  My rpms drop dramatically (for a moment) when I kick the A/C on.  BTW--Anyone know how to tighten an A/C compresser belt on a 2.8??  Mine must be lose (also still have some oil residue on it) since the belt squeals terribly with the A/C on.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||06-09-2001||11:17 PM||||Yeah, the drop in RPM is what <B>normal</B> engines do, becuase of the increased load.<P>mines just being difficult!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||06-09-2001||11:54 PM||||The ECM should open the IAC just a crack more, when the AC is turned on, in order to maintain the correct idle speed. It won't open the butterfly, though.<BR>With an automatic, it will also open the IAC a bit when you put it in gear, and close it a bit when you shift into neutral.<BR>Joe, I don't have a clue what's up with yours. Sorry. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-10-2001||12:50 PM||||87 and up Fiero 4 cyl uses a DA-V5 compressor. These units do not cycle on and off like the old Harrison A6 units. In fact they should not cycle at all most of the time. <P>These compressors are variable displacement and have an internal pressure valve that controls the displacement based on system requirements.<P>This has 2 big benifits...<P>No surging as the clutch cycles like the A6 offten caused. The load change is very smooth.<P>Better fuel economy since the compressor is matching itself to the needs of the rest of the AC system. As opposed to A6 that always runs at full load.||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||06-10-2001||05:33 PM||||Well I guess I'll just have to live with it and adapt.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroJoe||06-11-2001||04:23 PM||||Well folks, here's a follow up:<P>Today after school, I started her up, and she did the normal start-up idle thing.  Since the engine was cold, the lowest it would come down to is about 1300rpm.  So, I turned the AC on right then (still parked, car not moving).  It went up to 2k, and then came right back down!  I was sooo happy!  <P>I said "Good car, remember I have a sunroof swap for you, so how about you do this everytime I turn the AC on, it'd be very thoughtful of you"   Hehe, well not exactly, but I was still pretty happy.  So, I left the AC and started to drive home.  Once the engine got warmed up, it idled normally. <P>Once I got on the highway, I turned the AC off, took it out of gear, and the car idled at its normal.  So then, I turned the AC back on, and it didnt go way up to 3k like it did before!  It just went to about 2k and then dropped right back down.<P>So it's been a good day, I guess.  Maybe I'll treat her to a nice set of blue ion headlights...just got them in the mail today!||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-11-2001||07:10 PM||||You need to go over all the connectors and make sure they are clean. Including disconnect the battery and reseat the ECM connectors. One of them may be cruddy.<P>There's also the posibility the ECM itself is batty but that's a last resort item.||||reg||1
